.TH FDISK 8 "13 July 2026" "ReBSD" "ReBSD System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
fdisk \- inspect and edit an MBR partition table
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fdisk -p
.I device
.br
.B fdisk -d
.I device partition
.br
.B fdisk
.RB [ -w ]
.RB [ -t
.IR type ]
.B -n
.I device
.RI [ size-kbytes ]
.br
.B fdisk -a
.I device partition
.br
.B fdisk -T -t
.I type device partition
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fdisk
operates on the four primary entries in a classic 512-byte MBR.  It uses
explicit little-endian on-disk fields and validates the MBR signature,
partition bounds, integer overflow and overlapping partitions before writing.
.PP
The
.B -p
operation opens the device read-only.  Mutating operations write exactly one
512-byte MBR sector and then request that the kernel reload the partition
table.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B -p
Print the device size and all used partition entries.
.TP
.B -d
Delete the partition numbered 1 through 4.
.TP
.B -n
Create a partition after the highest existing partition.  If
.I size-kbytes
is omitted, use the remainder of the device.
.TP
.B -w
Initialize the MBR partition table before creating a new partition.  This
option is valid only with
.BR -n .
.TP
.B -a
Toggle the active flag on the selected partition.
.TP
.B -T
Change the selected partition type.  This operation requires
.BR -t .
.TP
.BI -t " type"
Set an MBR type byte, written in decimal or with a C-style base prefix such as
0x0c.  The default for a new partition is 0xb7, the ReBSD native filesystem
type.
.SH DEVICES
The common disk layer exposes a whole disk as
.B /dev/sd0
and its four primary MBR partitions as
.BR /dev/sd0a ,
.BR /dev/sd0b ,
.BR /dev/sd0c
and
.BR /dev/sd0d .
The same naming and ioctl interface is used regardless of whether the backing
transport is USB Mass Storage, SD/MMC, IDE or SATA.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Only classic MBR is supported.  GPT, extended/logical partitions and disks
requiring LBAs wider than 32 bits are not supported.
.PP
Writing requires a writable disk backend.  A read-only backend, including the
initial USB Mass Storage implementation, can be inspected but cannot be
partitioned.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkfs (8),
.BR mount (8)
